provenance.steps_response:
[
  {
    "description": "With both link partners configured for auto-negotiation on a single test link, bring the link up and confirm it establishes. Capture the resolved speed and duplex on both ports as evidence that negotiation settled on the highest rate and duplex the two ports mutually support.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "On an auto-configured copper port, establish the link first with straight-through cabling and then with crossover cabling between the DUT and its partner. Confirm the link comes up in both cases without manual polarity intervention, exercising automatic MDI/MDIX resolution.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Repeat auto/auto negotiation across each supported media type in turn — copper 10-Gig SFP, fixed copper 1-Gig, and 1-Gig fibre. For each medium, confirm the link establishes and settles at the highest rate that medium and the two ports mutually support.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Leave one port advertising auto-negotiation while pinning its partner to a fixed speed that forms a supported combination. Confirm the auto side converges on that fixed speed and the link establishes.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Leave one port advertising auto-negotiation while pinning its partner to a fixed duplex. Confirm the auto side resolves to a duplex compatible with the partner and the link establishes.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Negotiate the link at gigabit and higher rates and inspect what each port advertises and resolves for duplex. Confirm half-duplex is neither advertised nor resolved at those speeds and that only full-duplex results occur.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Pin both partners to fixed settings that are unsupported or mismatched for the link. Confirm the outcome is predictable — either no link or a link at a safe fallback rate — and that the port does not enter an inconsistent or oscillating state.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "After a link has negotiated, read the operational speed, duplex and negotiation state reported by each port. Confirm the reported values reflect the actually resolved link and that the two partners agree with one another.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "With a negotiated link between the DUT and a partner, examine the auto-negotiation support flag, enabled status, advertised capability and resulting operational MAU type both locally and, via the partner, through the exchanged LLDP IEEE 802.3 MAC/PHY TLVs. Confirm the local and remote views are consistent with the resolved link.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "On an established auto-negotiated link, change the media, speed or duplex on one side to force a renegotiation. Confirm the link re-resolves cleanly to the new highest mutually supported combination and that no stale speed, duplex or negotiation state persists on either partner.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  }
]

